      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Factory workers table"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Three factories record the number of men and women workers. When the numbers of two months are added, we add the matrices."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "addition and subtraction",
          "scalar multiplication",
          "properties of addition",
          "condition for multiplication",
          "row times column",
          "non-commutative",
          "identity and transpose"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Two matrices of the same order can be added or subtracted by adding or subtracting corresponding elements.\nMultiplying a matrix by a scalar k multiplies every element by k.\nMatrix addition is commutative and associative; the zero matrix is the additive identity and minus A is the additive inverse.\nMatrix A of order m by n can be multiplied with B of order n by p, and the product has order m by p.\nThe element in row i and column j of AB is the sum of products of row i of A with column j of B.\nMultiplication is generally not commutative: AB need not equal BA.\nThe identity matrix satisfies A I = I A = A, and the transpose of AB equals B transpose times A transpose."
        }
      },
